You smooth a seam of an article that retells a video, from its atoms: the units of what was said that a faithful retelling must keep. Each section of the article was written apart, so where one section ends and the next begins, the two paragraphs may not read on: a word said twice, a pronoun with nothing before it to point to, an opening that starts over. Rewrite the two so that a reader passing the heading between them reads on. Code checks every sentence you write against the atoms.

The rules:
1. Write the two paragraphs, in order, with a blank line between them. Write no heading: the article has it.
2. Each paragraph tells the atoms it is given, all of them, and no others. End each sentence with the markers of the atoms it tells: "… at four tons [A7]." Every sentence tells an atom: a sentence without a marker is not written.
3. Change only how things are said, and most where the paragraphs meet: how the first one ends and the second begins, what a pronoun or a link points to, a word said twice across the heading. The first still begins, and the second still ends, much as written: there they meet the rest of their sections.
4. Keep the words to quote word for word, between double quotes, and every number, name and term as the paragraphs have them.
5. Say each atom as it was said: a hedge stays a hedge, a strong statement stays strong, and who said it stays who said it.
6. Write only what the atoms say. A link that adds what was not said, a cause or a contrast no atom gives, is not written.

An example. The seam between section 2, "Loading the bridge", and section 3, "Why arches help".
Paragraph 1, the end of section 2. Its atoms:
A7 [1:02] number: The first bridge the team tried failed at four tons.
A8 [1:03] opinion, aside: The speaker was surprised that it failed.
As written:
The first bridge the team tried failed at four tons [A7]. That the bridge failed surprised the speaker [A8].
Paragraph 2, the start of section 3. Its atoms:
A9 [2:40] claim, said by Ada: Arches spread the load. Names: Ada.
As written:
She says that arches spread the load [A9].
Your answer:
The first bridge the team tried failed at four tons [A7], to the speaker's surprise [A8].

Arches, Ada says, spread the load [A9].

